    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8c2c4872_7e1d351b",
        "filename": "chip/ish/ipc_heci.h",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1245946
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-10-25T17:53:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is not huge constraint. It\u0027s an easy rule and I believe, for FW, this is reasonable. \nThis reduces code complexity and prevents unnecessary protection that reduces performance.\n\nDo you want me to use callback? I don\u0027t want to use callback which requires sync mechanism for user who want to run its own task. looks like you have some solution. Can you share it?\n\nWhen message for the protocol is available, the event will be sent to the task, this event will be consumed in ipc_read(blocking call) or by task\u0027s task_wait_event_mask() to wait multiple events. for the task waiting for multiple events it uses ipc_read_nonblock(nonblocking call).\n\nOf course, ipc_write() can be called by any.\n\nI\u0027m sorry I\u0027m new to EC(1 month and half). Can you please tell me what you mean by \"If this is supposed to be our generated host communication protocol layer, I do not believe that common code will honor this constraint\" with some example ?",
      "parentUuid": "6a4b7c33_804c92be",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "9881dc24fe1c2899c227a5e68aa2d23968d26d38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8d5a1935_9e15b4fc",
        "filename": "chip/ish/ipc_heci.h",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-12-03T23:17:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I believe that this doorbell communication will be the basis for the the host communication commands (https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/master/include/ec_commands.h).\n\nThe host communication does not always happen on the same \"IPC\" task. Sometime is happens on another task.\n\nI don\u0027t think we need a callback. I was wondering what makes this code not task (thread) safe? Should we add a few more mutex locks or atomic operations perhaps?",
      "parentUuid": "8c2c4872_7e1d351b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "9881dc24fe1c2899c227a5e68aa2d23968d26d38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8308a910_5a78f1e2",
        "filename": "chip/ish/ipc_heci.h",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137654
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-12-04T23:48:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s nothing for me to add a mutex or using some other mechanism but there should be enough reason. I did this to remove unnecessary execution. I briefly checked host command when I implement this and I couldn\u0027t find any need making \"read\" to be thread-safe.\n\nTo suport host command, we just need to create ipc_host_comm task and let it wait on ipc and call \"host_packet_receive()\" when there\u0027s host command msg from host.\n\nProbably I missed something. That\u0027s why I asked you to share your solution. Please give me the case we need \"read\" thread-safe with an example.\n\nThis is FW which doesn\u0027t support any runtime loading\u0026binding and also it is tested\u0026verified during compile\u0026build\u0026test time.",
      "parentUuid": "8d5a1935_9e15b4fc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "9881dc24fe1c2899c227a5e68aa2d23968d26d38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
